The Tide That Never Ends 

The console room hummed with the low, contented sigh of a TARDIS at rest — not idling, exactly, more like a great sleeping animal breathing between adventures. Amy Pond sat cross-legged on the jump seat, wringing seawater out of the hem of her cardigan, while the Doctor circled the console barefoot, leaving wet footprints that the ship seemed to be quietly, disapprovingly evaporating as fast as he made them. 

"I'm just saying," Amy said, "a planet that's entirely ocean. No coastline. No beach. No little umbrella in your drink because there's nowhere to put a bar." 

"There was a floating bar." 

"There was a raft with a fish on it who mixed cocktails with his tail." 

"Best mixologist in three galaxies, that fish." The Doctor flicked a switch, frowned at the readout it gave him, and flicked it back. "You're just annoyed you lost the swimming race." 

"I was winning the swimming race, until something the size of a bus decided I looked like lunch." 

"You looked delicious. I told you, that colour suits you." He grinned at her sideways, the kind of grin that always meant he was proud of himself for something only he found funny yet. "Besides, technically you didn't lose. You achieved a highly efficient tactical retreat." 

"I screamed and climbed you like a tree." 

"Like I said. Tactical." He straightened up, wiped his hands on his tweed — which did absolutely nothing, since his hands were still faintly turquoise from the planet's bioluminescent shallows — and beamed around the console room as if it were the finest view in the universe, which, depending on the day, it occasionally was. "Best fully-oceanic planet in the whole of the Local Group, that one. No name yet, as it happens. Native life hasn't invented one. Bit rude of us to have visited a whole world and not even know what to call it, really." 

"So name it." 

"Can't. Rules." He said this the way he said most rules — as though they were both sacred and enormously fun to have just broken. "Naming rights go to the first species to develop written language. They're only up to interpretive bubble-patterns at the moment. Give it another eleven million years." 

Amy opened her mouth to say something about patience not being either of their strong suits, when a sound cut across the console room — small, tinny, entirely wrong for a time machine the size of a cathedral inside a police box. A ring. An old-fashioned, insistent, brring-brring sort of ring, coming from the ancient black telephone bolted to the console, the one Amy had always assumed was decorative, in the way that everything on the TARDIS was decorative right up until the moment it very much wasn't. 

The Doctor went still. 

"Is that—" 

"It's the phone." He said it quietly, almost reverently, already moving. 

"I didn't know it actually worked." 

"Everything works. Eventually. That's rather the point of it." He picked up the receiver, and for a moment his whole face changed — the easy grin folding away into something older, more careful, the face of a man doing sums no one else could see. He didn't say hello. He never said hello to this particular caller; Amy would learn that soon enough. 

"Yes," the Doctor said instead, listening, nodding once at nothing, his knuckles pale around the receiver. A pause. Longer this time. His eyes flicked, just once, to Amy, and away again. "Yes. I understand. Hold tight — don't do anything until we get there." 

He set the phone down with a click that seemed far too loud for such a small, ordinary sound. 

"Doctor?" Amy was on her feet now, cardigan forgotten. "Who was that?" 

He was already at the console, already throwing levers, already wearing the particular expression he wore right before the floor started shaking and the world outside the doors stopped being safe. The great central column began to rise and fall, and the ancient engines groaned their familiar, impossible groan. 

"Old friend," he said. "Rather insistent one. Tends to be, when there's a war on." 

"A war? Whose war?" 

The Doctor didn't answer straightaway. He was too busy grinning again — but it was a different grin now, sharper at the edges, the one that meant he'd already decided this was going to be enormous, and possibly on fire. 

"Hold onto something, Pond," he said instead. "We're going to see Winston Churchill!" 

"The Winston Churchill?" Amy said, disbelief written all over her face. 

The Doctor just smiled and flipped the dematerialisation switch. The ground started shaking, and the TARDIS started up that humming noise — that deep, groaning, wheezing sound that meant the universe was about to be rearranged around them, whether it liked it or not. 

Questions started flowing through Amy's head, and she began asking them rapidly. How does Winston Churchill have your number? Does he know about the TARDIS? How did you two even meet? 

"I met him a long time ago," the Doctor said. "He's a friend" — not answering any of Amy's questions. 

The central column slowed its pumping, the wheezing groan deepening into something lower, heavier, almost tired — and then came the sound Amy had already learned to brace for: a series of scraping, shuddering thuds, like the TARDIS was dragging its heels on the way down. The lights along the console dimmed once, flickered, and steadied. 

They'd landed. 

The Doctor was already straightening his bow tie in the nearest reflective surface, looking entirely too pleased with himself for a man who'd just refused to answer three perfectly reasonable questions. 

"Well," he said, striding for the doors. "Best behaviour, Pond. Difficult year for old Winston, this one." 

"Which year?" 

He paused, hand on the door, and glanced back at her with that maddening half-smile. 

"Come and find out." 

He threw the doors open. 

Amy started walking rapidly toward them, excitement written all over her face — the kind of excitement that came with every single landing, no matter how many times she told herself she was used to it by now. She stopped dead in the doorway. 

They were standing on top of a building, wind cutting sharp and cold across the rooftop, and below them sprawled a city she almost didn't recognise. 1939, the Doctor had as good as said. Britain. And spread out beneath her in the grey light was mile after mile of wreckage — collapsed roofs, blackened brick, streets half-swallowed by rubble, the whole skyline scarred in a way no history book photograph had ever quite prepared her for. 

"Doctor," she said quietly, all the earlier questions gone out of her voice. "What happened here?" 

"War," the Doctor said, his face suddenly sad. 

He turned and started down a narrow flight of stairs without another word. Amy followed him, as she always did. 

When they reached the bottom, the place was chaos — hundreds of people talking over one another, papers changing hands, a current of panic running under everything like a live wire. This was the command centre of the war. 

 
